CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanisms are clear, but their real observable effects depend on which save paths execute and on runtime file contents—particularly comments, malformed JSON, omitted keys, and environment-variable references. Those conditions cannot be determined from this diff alone.

CLI settings persistence now parses and rewrites existing settings through comment-json, retaining comments and existing document structure while restoring tracked environment-variable references instead of always replacing the file with plain JSON. Loading now records environment substitutions per settings scope so later writes can avoid persisting resolved values.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When an existing valid settings file is saved, it is parsed, updated in place, and serialized through comment-json. This is intended to retain comments; keys not represented in the supplied update also remain in the document rather than being removed by a full-file replacement.
  • Settings originally expressed as a single environment-variable reference are tracked during loading and changed back to that reference when the corresponding scope is saved, avoiding persistence of the resolved string value in the ordinary case.
  • If an existing settings file cannot be parsed, a save now reports the parse problem and leaves the file unchanged instead of overwriting it with the current settings snapshot.

Investigate
  • Trace every caller that saves or mutates settings, especially migrations, reset/remove operations, and callers that expect the persisted object to be a complete replacement rather than a patch.
  • Inspect environment substitution across objects, arrays, dotted property names, and user edits made after loading; compare the mapping lifetime with the lifetime of a LoadedSettings instance.
  • Verify the concrete comment-json behavior for comments, ordering, duplicate keys, trailing commas, formatting, and parser failures against all settings-file syntax accepted elsewhere in the CLI.
  • Identify the system, user, and workspace file boundaries, including trust checks, symlinks, concurrent writers, and whether writes need atomic replacement.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not establish which CLI commands or other callers invoke LoadedSettings.setValue or call saveSettings directly, so the user-facing reach of the new persistence behavior needs call-graph investigation.
  • The concrete system, user, and workspace paths, their ownership and trust rules, and whether other processes write the same files are not shown.
  • Whether deployed settings contain environment references inside arrays is unknown; that runtime content determines whether the array restoration gap can expose resolved values.
  • Whether real settings files contain comments, malformed syntax, or keys omitted by later settings snapshots is unknown, so the observable frequency of the preservation and retention behavior cannot be established here.
  • The exact preservation of comment placement, property ordering, accepted JSON extensions, and formatting depends on comment-json behavior beyond what the supplied implementation and tests establish.

